def find_Max(arr, start, end):
    # Base case: if the array is not rotated
    if end == 0:
        return arr[0]

    # Find the index of the minimum element
    pivot = start + (end - start) // 2

    # If the element at pivot is greater than the last element, the max must be in the right part
    if arr[pivot] > arr[end]:
        return find_Max(arr, pivot, end)
    else:
        # The max must be in the left part
        return find_Max(arr, start, pivot)
```
This function uses a binary search approach to efficiently find the maximum element in a sorted and rotated array. It first checks for the base case where the array is not rotated. If it is not rotated, the first element is the maximum element. Otherwise, it finds the index of the minimum element using binary search. The max must be in the right or left part depending on the element at the pivot compared to the last element. This approach ensures that the time complexity is O(log n).

